00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:02.320
Leah: What is Agile development?

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.980
Chris: Let’s have a look at this in more detail.

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:12.119
Leah: ‘Agile Development’ which is also more commonly
known as Agile Software Development, is an umbrella

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:18.810
term for a range of frameworks, such as ‘Scrum’,
and practices such as the ‘daily stand-up meeting’.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:25.029
It adopts the principles set out in the ‘Agile
Manifesto’ and the ‘Agile 12 Principles’.

00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:32.479
Chris: The Agile Manifesto is a short document, which
was created in 2001 from a group of leading professionals

00:00:32.769 --> 00:00:35.389
who met to discuss the future of software development.

00:00:35.949 --> 00:00:41.440
Its focus is on value to individuals and
interactions, working software, customer

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.810
collaboration and responding to change.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:49.769
The Manifesto itself is short and made up
of 68 words and has since been embraced by

00:00:49.769 --> 00:00:55.890
many different teams and companies, and it is
agreed by many that it is still relevant today.

00:00:57.010 --> 00:01:01.870
Leah: The 12 Principles of Agile Software,
expand on the values within the Manifesto.

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:09.990
The focus is on the customer, continuous delivery
of product, change, frequency, team collaboration,

00:01:10.049 --> 00:01:18.839
motivation, support, communication, measurement,
excellence, simplicity, self-organisation, and reflection.

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:26.450
Agile came about as a process to address issues
that software developers faced, focussing on

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:32.200
a project being flexible, so it could respond
to any changes and deal with any uncertainty.

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:39.019
Chris: Agile is people-oriented and result-focused in
respect to software delivery and instead of delivering

00:01:39.059 --> 00:01:45.490
all the product at the end of the project, the software is
built incrementally from the start in time-boxed 'sprints'.

00:01:46.729 --> 00:01:52.800
It is an iterative approach used in software
development projects with a focus on continuous releases

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:55.850
that takes customer feedback into consideration.

00:01:56.690 --> 00:02:01.760
Development tasks are broken down into
bite size chunks, which can be achieved

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:04.299
in a certain period known as a ‘sprint’.

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:08.509
Each task would be assigned a
measurement of time or complexity.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:15.930
The self-organised agile team itself would normally
consist of different skillsets, working independently and

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:19.710
collaboratively, to achieve the tasks within that set time.

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:26.010
Leah: Agile development’s iterative approach means
that continuous testing and continuous improvement

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:33.369
takes place throughout the entire product lifecycle
this gives early warnings of quality issues, reduces

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:39.459
the risk of failure, and gives opportunities to
find new ways to fix those issues and or processes.

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:44.760
There is an emphasis on frequent continuous
releases with customer transparency

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:47.400
and visibility on the work in progress.

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:52.660
Chris: Project planning is also ongoing
throughout the project which is known as

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:56.309
just-in-time [also a method used in ‘LEAN’].

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:02.489
Estimates for each stage or ‘Sprint’ are relative
and these are refined as the project progresses.

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:06.710
Features also begin more general
and details are added later.

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:13.069
As not all the details are available at the beginning
of the project, features being added later are often

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:16.280
influenced by other members or the team and the customer.

